text:
The strongest name triggers are platform and feature fit. OneNote is a frequent cross-platform and Android/Windows suggestion, and Microsoft documents its Android handwriting support. Goodnotes is a natural match for iPad, stylus, and handwriting-to-text prompts, including Android and Windows queries. Notability is especially likely for student, lecture, recording, and transcription prompts. Notion fits work, research, and general-purpose notes; its current AI meeting notes also make it relevant to meeting queries. Otter is more likely when the prompt points to recording, dictation, or lectures than for everyday notes. ([Microsoft Support](https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/onenote/take-handwritten-notes-in-onenote-for-android), [Goodnotes Support](https://support.goodnotes.com/hc/en-us/articles/7922907940367-Frequently-requested-features-for-Android-Windows-and-Web), [Notability](https://notability.com/meetings), [Notion](https://www.notion.com/help/ai-meeting-notes))

Across the full set, the explicit Goodnotes and Notability prompts guarantee mentions, while many niche-device and off-topic prompts dilute the rates. My estimates for the share of answers naming each app, averaged across all 100 questions, are:

{"OneNote":42,"Goodnotes":31,"Notability":24,"Notion":36,"Otter":12}
